WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:01.939 --> 00:00:10.309
We turn now to 111 and I am going to ask Barbara
to introduce her paper on assuring operational

00:00:10.309 --> 00:00:11.309
delivery for 111.

00:00:11.309 --> 00:00:12.570
Assuring NHS 111 Operational Delivery
Barbara Hakin

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:13.579
National Director: Commissioning Development
Preamble

00:00:13.579 --> 00:00:14.579
Thank you, Chairman.

00:00:14.579 --> 00:00:18.600
It is interesting that many of the papers
we have had this morning have been introduced

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:27.730
by the watershed moment of April 1st, and
that is certainly the same for the real change

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:49.110
to the way that services for patients when
they took over on April 1st, was that of the

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:50.530
NHS 111 service.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:57.609
Of course, at that point we would take on
our role as the oversight of CCGs in supporting

00:00:57.609 --> 00:01:01.629
them and commissioning the best services for
their patients.

00:01:01.629 --> 00:01:09.030
The paper is designed to give the Board an
update on where we are with 111 and seek the

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:12.780
Board's approval for the way forward.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:16.660
111
It would only be appropriate for me to start

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:22.960
this discussion acknowledging the problems
that we have had with performance and some

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:30.310
providers who have not been able to meet the
key standards the commissioner set for them,

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:36.810
and not been able to answer the phone to patients
quickly enough and respond quickly enough.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:43.210
That has been, undeniably, unacceptable in
many places -- not in many places, sorry,

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:47.930
I will rephrase that -- that has been unacceptable
in some places.

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:53.750
I do think that in all of that it is really
important that we remember that in most places

00:01:53.750 --> 00:02:00.380
111 has provided a very good service to patients
for virtually all of the time, and that 111

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:05.940
has made a real difference to patients who
have had difficulties and needed someone to

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:12.590
help them navigate the system, and needed
to help them to receive medical and clinical

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:14.850
health and advice.

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:20.790
The service was designed to prevent the confusion
that I think a lot of patients had as to who

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:27.870
they turned to, mostly commonly outside normal
working hours when their GP surgery is not

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:32.730
open, and when the patients did not think
that dialling 999 was appropriate because

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:37.069
they felt that the condition that they had
was not that urgent, and there were a plethora

00:02:37.069 --> 00:02:40.640
of numbers that they could ring and people
that they could turn to.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:46.920
111 is designed to replace all that with a
single, simple, 'You can dial 111 and you

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:48.590
can get the advice you need'.

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:54.410
As I say, for the most part, people are receiving
good and prompt advice, so there are lots

00:02:54.410 --> 00:03:00.020
of stories we hear about where 111 has made
a real difference.

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:05.459
My own daughter, who has a new baby a couple
of weeks ago, dialled 111 when the baby was

00:03:05.459 --> 00:03:09.720
not well -- fortunately it was not anything
very serious -- and she received a fantastic

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:10.850
service.

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:16.930
The phone was answered quickly, she was given
the right advice because baby was fine, and

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:23.260
she was booked into a local out-of-hours clinic
quickly and swiftly.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:29.080
We hear of patients who decided they would
ring 111 when they might not have done anything

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:30.080
else.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:32.980
They might not have bothered to seek medical
advice because they had what they thought

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:37.670
was indigestion, and it transpired that actually
this patient was having a heart attack and

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:42.739
an ambulance could be dispatched immediately,
and that made a huge difference to that patient.

00:03:42.739 --> 00:03:48.930
I think the most compelling story for me is
something that happened near to us in Yorkshire,

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:56.110
in Huddersfield -- the parents chose to go
public on this -- where parents of a child

00:03:56.110 --> 00:04:02.810
were concerned that the child was not very
well but were not desperately anxious at that

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:03.810
time.

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:09.020
They rang 111 and the call handler on 111
was able, through talking to the patient,

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:14.569
to recognise that the child was potentially,
very seriously ill, despatched an ambulance

00:04:14.569 --> 00:04:15.810
immediately.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:20.420
The child, who is now well I am very pleased
to say, did turn out to have a very serious

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:26.530
illness -- had meningitis -- and the hospital
doctor said that the swift and prompt action

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:30.660
of getting that patient to the hospital was
what made all the difference.

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:33.200
Standards
We must get this service right but we must

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:39.120
not underestimate the advantages to this service
because, unlike its predecessors, it is able

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:45.040
to dispatch ambulance, make appointments for
patients and put them immediately in contact

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:47.920
with the medical care that they require.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:53.930
However, as I said, some of our providers
let us down very badly.

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:58.910
The standards that we look for, for patients,
are gold standards.

00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:04.630
We measure our standards on two main indicators:
how quickly the phone call is answered and

00:05:04.630 --> 00:05:10.740
the number of patients that abandon the phone
call and put the phone down.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:15.160
The standards in the contracts that the CCGs
have negotiated are gold standard.

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:22.660
They are higher than standards that we have
expected in previous, similar services.

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:29.200
As I said, for the most part, across the whole
country which is covered by 111, during the

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:34.450
week those gold standards are being met by
pretty much all providers.

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:40.639
At weekends, over the weekends, we have had
some significant problems with some providers

00:05:40.639 --> 00:05:47.120
really having very, very poor response times;
patients waiting completely unacceptable lengths

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:49.880
of times and too many patients abandoning
those calls.

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:53.150
Improvements
Now, over the last few weeks NHS England has

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:58.600
worked with the CCGs to work with providers
to make sure that the service is safe and

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:04.390
the best it can be, and we have seen a very
significant improvement.

00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:11.010
Three or four weekends ago, the Easter weekend,
where the standards in a number of places

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:17.790
were very poor, through to last weekend where
for 90% of the country that is covered by

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:23.090
111, the gold standards were being met even
at the weekend.

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:24.889
That does not mean we are complacent.

00:06:24.889 --> 00:06:27.330
We have another Bank Holiday coming up.

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:31.330
We have put a lot in place to achieve those
improvements.

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:35.720
As I say, we have worked with providers who
have taken on more call handlers.

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:42.130
We have put in place contingency, back up
whereby other organisations or other groups

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:48.820
who could handle these calls, including the
original out of hours and 0845 number could

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:51.690
be switched back on, to just manage the risk.

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:57.199
We will be doing the same over the Bank Holiday
weekend to make sure our patients get a great

00:06:57.199 --> 00:06:58.250
service.

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:02.430
90% of the country is covered by 111.

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:07.419
We only have 10% of the country, slightly
over 10% of the country where we need to rollout

00:07:07.419 --> 00:07:08.479
111.

00:07:08.479 --> 00:07:14.770
Again, we have put in place a process whereby
there will be no further rollout of 111 in

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:21.580
any area until, working with our CCGs and
nationally across the country, we in NHS England

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:25.900
are satisfied that that rollout can be delivered
safely.

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:35.830
Also, we are working with our CCGs in order
to take actions with providers where the service

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:37.300
has been unsatisfactory.

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:46.300
NHS England will not shy away from working
with our CCGs to support them to invoke the

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:52.620
financial penalties where services have not
been the standard for patients, or indeed,

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:56.980
to revoke the contracts where we believe that
a provider cannot show that they are going

00:07:56.980 --> 00:08:00.610
to be able to improve on the current standards
and make sure that they deliver the sorts

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:02.580
of services our patients want.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:07.009
A sustainable service
That is the position we are currently with

00:08:07.009 --> 00:08:09.690
performance on 111.

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:15.910
I think that we now have to move forward and
make sure that we have a fantastic and sustainable

00:08:15.910 --> 00:08:18.570
service over the coming months and years.

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:25.150
There are a couple things that we are doing
in order to make sure that is the case.

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:31.539
We will want to look at precisely the nature
of 111 in the future, how it is commissioned

00:08:31.539 --> 00:08:34.260
and the balance between national and local.

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:36.969
The scope of 111 -- have we got the scope
right?

00:08:36.969 --> 00:08:44.219
Is the potential for 111 to do more as a direct
service to patients?

00:08:44.219 --> 00:08:48.649
The clinical input: are we absolutely certain
that the balance between lay call handling

00:08:48.649 --> 00:08:51.860
and clinical input is right in all the call
centres?

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:57.190
The shape of provision: we have 46 sites at
the moment with a number of different providers

00:08:57.190 --> 00:09:01.150
so are we confident that that shape of provision
is appropriate?

00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:06.320
We will, over the next months bring regular
reports to the Board.

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:12.840
We have a programme oversight group, including
our CCGs which will look at all those aspects.

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:38.839
In addition, the Board asked last time that
we put in place [break in audio] -- and again,

00:09:38.839 --> 00:09:52.160
that 
the lessons learned from the national coordination

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:57.220
versus local sensitivity, both of which are
important so that review will look at all

00:09:57.220 --> 00:09:58.220
of those things.

00:09:58.220 --> 00:10:00.680
Q&amp;A
Malcolm Grant: And that will be a review conducted

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:02.240
by an external reviewer?

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:04.820
Would you like to add anything to that, Bill?

00:10:04.820 --> 00:10:07.899
Bill McCarthy: No; I think Barbara has covered
it very well.

00:10:07.899 --> 00:10:15.470
We are committed to learning so we need to
learn how best to do the most complex, large

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:16.470
projects.

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:23.110
An awful lot of this we took the baton on
at a fairly late stage but there will be times

00:10:23.110 --> 00:10:31.029
in the future where similarly complex challenges
we will be taking forward so we should use

00:10:31.029 --> 00:10:36.440
this opportunity to get ourselves into really
smart shape to do those.

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:45.420
I have been talking to an independent, external
person but I do not have that name for you

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:48.020
today, I am afraid.

00:10:48.020 --> 00:10:51.920
Malcolm Grant: Thank you for that.

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:52.920
Moira?

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:55.170
Moira Gibb: Thanks, Malcolm.

00:10:55.170 --> 00:11:00.980
Obviously, the public have had a picture of
a bad service even if they have not experienced

00:11:00.980 --> 00:11:05.300
it directly; what they have read in the newspapers
or heard about it is probably giving them

00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:07.960
a very negative impression.

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:17.760
Who is responsible for the information flow
to the public about using it, when to use

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:20.000
it, how to use it, etc?

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:22.290
Is that us or individual CCGs?

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:30.990
Is there a thought, whoever it is, of doing
something that helps people understand again,

00:11:30.990 --> 00:11:36.570
hopefully, not dissuading people from using,
for example, when it is appropriate for them?

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:38.279
Barbara Hakin: Thank you.

00:11:38.279 --> 00:11:40.230
I think we both have a responsibility.

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:45.930
I think CCGs have a responsibility to communicate
with their patients locally, but I think NHS

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:51.390
has an important role in the national message
in helping and supporting patients.

00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:57.210
First and foremost, we have to ensure that
this service is gold standard everywhere because

00:11:57.210 --> 00:12:02.430
I think then that speaks for itself; that
is how patient's confidence and the public's

00:12:02.430 --> 00:12:04.100
confidence will grow.

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:09.990
I do think we have an important role, firstly
in reassuring the public nationally that this

00:12:09.990 --> 00:12:15.140
is a really very good service, for the most
part, which does save lives.

00:12:15.140 --> 00:12:20.250
I think we also have an important role in
being clear to patients what they should do,

00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:24.089
because I think that a lot of this has created
some confusion for patients, 'Who should I

00:12:24.089 --> 00:12:26.250
ring and when'?

00:12:26.250 --> 00:12:29.080
The message is simple.

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:34.500
At the moment, in areas where we have 111
working, as I say which is almost 90% of the

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:42.150
country, as a safety net we still have the
0845 number and the GP out of hours number.

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:44.130
They are switched through to 111.

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:49.500
At the moment, if you are in that 90% of the
country where 111 is working, it does not

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:54.839
matter which of those numbers you ring you
will get through to a call handler in 111.

00:12:54.839 --> 00:12:59.940
For the 10% of patients who still are not
covered, if they were to ring 111 they will

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:04.290
receive an answer phone machine saying, '111
is not operational yet' and giving them the

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:08.660
numbers to ring -- obviously, if they use
0845 or their GP out-of-hours then that, for

00:13:08.660 --> 00:13:11.070
the moment, continues the same.

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:16.980
Our ambition continues to be that over the
next few months we have 111 nationally available.

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:19.500
Malcolm Grant: Margaret?

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:25.390
Margaret Casely-Hayford: Barbara, thank you
for the explanation of the oversight programme,

00:13:25.390 --> 00:13:31.700
which I think is going to be absolutely critical
to making sure that the providers who have

00:13:31.700 --> 00:13:37.370
not met the standard, actually begin to improve
and that we gain assurance on the improvement.

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:41.529
It was also really helpful to hear that there
are sanctions available to you.

00:13:41.529 --> 00:13:51.570
Will you actually publish the sanctions or
somehow inform us of what happens if there

00:13:51.570 --> 00:13:53.779
has been the ultimate failure?

00:13:53.779 --> 00:13:57.810
Barbara Hakin: We certainly will report that
to the Board.

00:13:57.810 --> 00:14:05.279
The contracts with providers are with CCGs
and those contracts are available, and an

00:14:05.279 --> 00:14:08.470
understanding of the penalties in those.

00:14:08.470 --> 00:14:14.470
I know that in certain areas those financial
penalties have been significantly brought

00:14:14.470 --> 00:14:16.519
into play.

00:14:16.519 --> 00:14:20.010
The problem with that, of course, is that
it does not help our patients get the better

00:14:20.010 --> 00:14:25.760
services, which is what we need, and we will
constantly, I think, in terms of looking at

00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:31.550
providers, have to measure the balance between
continuing to support a provider in order

00:14:31.550 --> 00:14:37.360
that they can deliver a better service, but
at the same time looking at an alternative

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:39.790
and being sure that an alternative would be
an improvement.

00:14:39.790 --> 00:14:44.360
I am happy to report to the Board, with the
very small number of providers, a couple of

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:50.589
providers who have had some seriously poor
performances, how discussions with them and

00:14:50.589 --> 00:14:54.290
their CCGs supported by us are going, and
the outcomes.

00:14:54.290 --> 00:14:55.389
Malcolm Grant: Victor?

00:14:55.389 --> 00:14:57.260
Victor Adebowale: Two questions really.

00:14:57.260 --> 00:15:03.130
One, are we measuring public perception and
the impact of the disproportionate -- and

00:15:03.130 --> 00:15:07.950
it is a disproportionate view -- that the
public are getting a bad service on the usage

00:15:07.950 --> 00:15:08.950
of 111?

00:15:08.950 --> 00:15:13.350
Are we measuring that going forward because
public perception may well affect usage?

00:15:13.350 --> 00:15:20.570
Are we also measuring the use of 111 going
forward by demographics?

00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:31.550
Are some areas using it more than others?

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:40.760
Some people are using it more than others
because that will tell us, in a sense, quite

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:45.970
a lot about whether it is having an impact
on improving healthcare.

00:15:45.970 --> 00:15:52.190
Barbara Hakin: The use of 111 is rising because,
of course, one of the ways that 111 was introduced

00:15:52.190 --> 00:15:59.470
-- it was a soft launch so that actually,
where the 0845 and the out-of-hours calls

00:15:59.470 --> 00:16:08.470
were switched over until the service settled
in, and then locally, the CCGs could advertise

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:11.740
this service to their patients.

00:16:11.740 --> 00:16:19.220
Of course, the recent media interest in this
means that many, many more patients are aware

00:16:19.220 --> 00:16:23.240
of 111; it has had a very high profile.

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:28.380
We will be doing everything we can to get
the message across that, for the most part,

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:33.630
patients get a really good service from 111,
and give patients the confidence which is

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:38.050
absolutely vital that patients have confidence
in the service, and for the most part they

00:16:38.050 --> 00:16:39.730
should have that confidence.

00:16:39.730 --> 00:16:45.430
I would hope we could perhaps rely on media
colleagues to help us with that message about

00:16:45.430 --> 00:16:49.139
the areas where the service is working really
well, because there are parts of the country

00:16:49.139 --> 00:16:52.320
where this service has been exemplary throughout.

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:56.980
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much for that
report, Barbara.

00:16:56.980 --> 00:17:02.520
This is an absolutely critical service and
I think for all the reasons that Barbara has

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:03.520
outlined.

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:08.720
There is the potential here for 111 to be
quite transformational in the way in which

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:10.859
NHS improves its responsiveness to patients.

00:17:10.859 --> 00:17:15.629
Tim, it would be useful, would it not, to
be able to understand how we could reflect

00:17:15.629 --> 00:17:18.909
on the dashboard the level of demand on 111?

00:17:18.909 --> 00:17:21.289
It is another important indicator of public
engagement.

00:17:21.289 --> 00:17:29.320
I am going to ask the Board if you would please
endorse the operational delivery plan, the

00:17:29.320 --> 00:17:34.220
setting up of the external review which I
think is critical for the reasons that Bill

00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:39.769
mentioned, not just to learn lessons of what
went well and what did not go so well with

00:17:39.769 --> 00:17:45.470
111, but how we would go about such a major
project in the future when it arises for us

00:17:45.470 --> 00:17:51.019
to do that; then finally, also to endorse
the longer-term strategy for the development

00:17:51.019 --> 00:17:52.019
of 111.

00:17:52.019 --> 00:17:53.049
Are we content to do that?

00:17:53.049 --> 00:17:54.049
We are?

00:17:54.049 --> 00:17:54.389
Thank you very much.

